In article <HAIRSTON.90May2184913@henry.ece.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
>as you can tell, i don't think a feature of the game should be the
>player's ignorance.

By that token, all monster descriptions should describe their special
attacks, defenses, AC, hit-dice, etc.  You wouldn't want a player to
be ignorant, would you?  Of course this would take most of the
surprises out of the game, as would giving a player a list of things
devoutly to be wished for.  Aye, there's the rub.

Hairston writes:
>
>let's suppose i know what i want and can't spell it correctly,

I agree with David here.  Nethack is inconsistent in handling wishes.
Sometimes it says "I have never heard of such things."  Other times it
gives you something random.  In the grey/gray case, nethack should
give you what you obviously want; the code existed at one time, right?
When you wish for blessed +3 dragon scale armor, nethack could at
least say that it has not heard of such things.  Giving you blessed +3
eelskin boots is not fair.  It shouldn't take much effort to do a pattern
match and give you the dragon scale mail in this case.

Gilbert writes (about wishes)
>
>[] genies don't want to give them out and give out tricked ones
>[] if they can.  there is a famous saying "Be careful what you wish for . . .
>[] you may get it!

This is a *great* idea.  There should be different kinds of genies
corresponding to the different kinds of classical wishes.

(0) the kind we have now

(1) contrary: gives you the opposite of what you want

(2) literal: gives you exactly what you want, but goes by the sound
rather than the spelling.  "So, you really want me to *raze* all of
your abilities?"

(3) convenient: gives you what you want be taking it from the most
convenient source.  If you have 3 amulets of lifesaving and a potion
of holy water, it dips one of your amulets of lifesaving in the holy
water and then gives you your own blessed amulet.  If you wish for a
blessed longsword named Excalibur it steals one from a hacker named
Bonga who shows up ten turns later looking for it.

(etc.) see gaming magazines for the other kinds of wishes.

This would really add balance and fun to the game.

